We need to find the remainder when \(7^{103}\) is divided by 23.
7^{103} = 7 (7^{102}) = 7 (7^{3})^{34} = 7 (343)^{34}
Now, we find the remainder of 343 when divided by 23:
343 = 23 \times 15 - 2 = 345 - 2
So, \(343 \equiv -2 \pmod{23}\). Substituting this back into the expression:
7^{103} \equiv 7 (-2)^{34} \pmod{23}
7^{103} \equiv 7 \cdot 2^{34} \pmod{23}
We can write \(7^{103}\) in the form \(23K_{1} + 7 \cdot 2^{34}\).
Now, let's evaluate \(7 \cdot 2^{34}\) modulo 23:
7 \cdot 2^{34} = 7 \cdot 2^{2} \cdot 2^{32} = 28 \cdot (2^{8})^{4} = 28 \cdot (256)^{4}
Next, find the remainder of 28 and 256 when divided by 23:
28 = 23 + 5 \implies 28 \equiv 5 \pmod{23}
256 = 23 \times 11 + 3 \implies 256 \equiv 3 \pmod{23}
Substitute these into the expression:
28 \cdot (256)^{4} \equiv 5 \cdot (3)^{4} \pmod{23}
5 \cdot 3^{4} = 5 \cdot 81
Now, find the remainder of 81 when divided by 23:
81 = 23 \times 3 + 12 \implies 81 \equiv 12 \pmod{23}
Substitute this back:
5 \cdot 81 \equiv 5 \cdot 12 \pmod{23}
5 \cdot 12 = 60
Finally, find the remainder of 60 when divided by 23:
60 = 23 \times 2 + 14 \implies 60 \equiv 14 \pmod{23}
Thus, \(7^{103} \equiv 14 \pmod{23}\).
The remainder is 14.

