For non-negative integers s and r, let
\binom{s}{r} = \begin{cases} \frac{s!}{r!(s-r)!} & \text{if } r \le s, \\ 0 & \text{if } r > s. \end{cases}
For positive integers m and n, let
g(m,n) = \sum_{p=0}^{m+n} \frac{f(m,n,p)}{\binom{n+p}{p}}
where for any nonnegative integer p,
f(m,n,p) = \sum_{i=0}^{p} \binom{m}{i} \binom{n+i}{p} \binom{p+n}{p-i}
Then which of the following statements is/are TRUE?

questions.explanation:
We are given the definitions:
f(m,n,p) = \sum_{i=0}^{p} \binom{m}{i} \binom{n+i}{p} \binom{p+n}{p-i}
g(m,n) = \sum_{p=0}^{m+n} \frac{f(m,n,p)}{\binom{n+p}{p}}

### Step 1: Simplify the product of binomial coefficients in f(m,n,p)

\binom{n+i}{p} \binom{p+n}{p-i} = \frac{(n+i)!}{p!(n+i-p)!} \times \frac{(p+n)!}{(p-i)!(p+n-(p-i))!}
= \frac{(n+i)!}{p!(n+i-p)!} \times \frac{(p+n)!}{(p-i)!(n+i)!}
= \frac{(p+n)!}{p!(p-i)!(n+i-p)!}
= \frac{(p+n)!}{p!n!} \times \frac{n!}{(p-i)!(n+i-p)!}
= \binom{n+p}{p} \binom{n}{p-i}

### Step 2: Substitute the simplified expression back into f(m,n,p)

So, \(f(m,n,p)\) becomes:
f(m,n,p) = \sum_{i=0}^{p} \binom{m}{i} \binom{n+p}{p} \binom{n}{p-i}
Since \(\binom{n+p}{p}\) is independent of \(i\), we can take it out of the summation:
f(m,n,p) = \binom{n+p}{p} \sum_{i=0}^{p} \binom{m}{i} \binom{n}{p-i}

### Step 3: Apply Vandermonde's Identity

Vandermonde's Identity states that \(\sum_{k=0}^{r} \binom{m}{k} \binom{n}{r-k} = \binom{m+n}{r}\). Applying this to the sum:
\sum_{i=0}^{p} \binom{m}{i} \binom{n}{p-i} = \binom{m+n}{p}
Therefore,
f(m,n,p) = \binom{n+p}{p} \binom{m+n}{p}

### Step 4: Substitute f(m,n,p) into g(m,n) and simplify

g(m,n) = \sum_{p=0}^{m+n} \frac{\binom{n+p}{p} \binom{m+n}{p}}{\binom{n+p}{p}}
g(m,n) = \sum_{p=0}^{m+n} \binom{m+n}{p}
This is the sum of all binomial coefficients for \(m+n\), which is \(2^{m+n}\).
g(m,n) = 2^{m+n}

### Step 5: Check each option

(A) \(g(m,n)=g(n,m)\)
\(g(m,n) = 2^{m+n}\) and \(g(n,m) = 2^{n+m}\). Since \(m+n = n+m\), this statement is TRUE.
(B) \(g(m,n+1)=g(m+1,n)\)
\(g(m,n+1) = 2^{m+(n+1)} = 2^{m+n+1}\). \(g(m+1,n) = 2^{(m+1)+n} = 2^{m+n+1}\). This statement is TRUE.
(C) \(g(2m,2n)=2g(m,n)\)
\(g(2m,2n) = 2^{2m+2n}\). \(2g(m,n) = 2 \cdot 2^{m+n} = 2^{m+n+1}\). Since \(2^{2m+2n} \ne 2^{m+n+1}\) for all positive integers \(m,n\) (it would only be equal if \(2m+2n = m+n+1 \implies m+n=1\)), this statement is FALSE.
(D) \(g(2m,2n)=(g(m,n))^2\)
\(g(2m,2n) = 2^{2m+2n}\). \((g(m,n))^2 = (2^{m+n})^2 = 2^{2(m+n)} = 2^{2m+2n}\). This statement is TRUE.
